# Siciliano in G minor, from the Flute Sonata in E-flat major, BWV 1031
# (attributed to J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a slow siciliana, the pastoral dance in a rocking compound
# time with the dotted "Sicilian" figure (eighth dotted, sixteenth,
# eighth) at the start of the tune. A long-breathed, singing flute line
# over a gently murmuring accompaniment: tender, never dragging.
#
# Source and authorship: the sonata survives in a copy by C. P. E. Bach
# and another from Bach's pupil C. F. Penzel, both naming J. S. Bach.
# Its authorship has been questioned (some scholars propose C. P. E.
# Bach; Jeanne Swack suggested it was modelled on a Quantz sonata),
# and IMSLP also lists it under C. P. E. Bach (H. 545), while Robert
# Marshall defends J. S. Bach on the strength of those two sources. The
# question concerns the whole sonata, not this movement alone.
#
# This Mutopia edition is "a new adaptation from the Bach-Gesellschaft"
# for flute and guitar: the guitar takes the keyboard's right-hand
# sixteenths and the bass. MIDI track 1 is the flute, track 2 the
# guitar (two voices).
#
# Time signature and bars: 6/8 in this edition, 33 bars (32 and the
# final chord), no upbeat and no repeats; the MIDI has the same 33 bars.
#
# Tempo: dotted quarter = 30 (eighth = 90), 66 beats, about 2:15.
# Track times for flute and keyboard (MusicBrainz recording search):
# Stephen Preston with Trevor Pinnock 2:15, about 29; Lisa Beznosiuk and
# Paul Nicholson 2:21, about 28; Aurele Nicolet and Karl Richter 2:18,
# about 29; Jean-Pierre Rampal 2:08 to 2:33 in different recordings,
# about 26 to 31; Andras Adorjan 1:53, about 35. Solo piano
# arrangements (Kempff 3:38) are much slower and do not count here. 30
# is the middle of the flute recordings; slower, the chip's held flute
# notes would fade before the phrase ends.
#
# Structure (from the Mutopia score, checked against the MIDI):
#   1-4    the tune in G minor, flute over guitar arpeggios.
#   5-9    it spins on and turns to B-flat major (cadence, bar 9).
#   10-15  the tune again in B-flat major, then through E-flat and C
#          minor.
#   16-21  toward D minor: bars 17 and 18 are the same phrase twice
#          (an echo), cadence in D minor at 21.
#   22     the flute rests; the guitar alone leads back.
#   23-26  the tune returns in G minor.
#   27-28  another phrase stated twice (an echo).
#   29-33  the close in G minor.

tempo 30 4.          # dotted quarter, two beats to the 6/8 bar
parts 1 2            # the flute first: when four notes sound, a guitar
                     # note gives way, never the tune

dyn 1 mp             # bars 1-9: the tune, quietly
broaden 9 0.94       # the cadence in B-flat
dyn 10 mf            # bars 10-16: the tune in B-flat, a little brighter
dyn 17 mf            # bar 17: the phrase
dyn 18 p             # bar 18: the same phrase echoed
dyn 19 mp            # bars 19-21: toward D minor
broaden 21 0.92      # the cadence in D minor
dyn 22 p             # bar 22: the guitar alone
dyn 23 mp            # bars 23-26: the tune returns
dyn 27 mf            # bar 27: the phrase
dyn 28 p             # bar 28: echoed
dyn 29 mp            # bars 29-33: the close

rit 2 0.7            # bars 32-33 into the final G
